NAME
    sort - sort lines of text

SYNOPSIS
    sort [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
    Reads the files named (as one stream; globs expand, "-" is stdin),
    or stdin, into memory, sorts the lines in ascending byte order (A-Z
    before a-z; equal lines keep their order) and prints them. CR is
    dropped; each file ends a line. sort is /BIN/SORT. stdin is the
    shell's < file or pipe (see shell), else the console up to Ctrl-D.

LIMITS
    200 lines of 79 characters: longer lines are cut, and the lines
    past the 200th are dropped, with "sort: more than 200 lines, the
    rest dropped" at the end.

EXAMPLES
    sort /FRUIT2.TXT               the fruit in alphabetical order
    sort /FRUIT.TXT /FRUIT2.TXT    the lines of both, merged
    sort                           typed lines, sorted at Ctrl-D

NOTES
    Removed from the P8X version: input from a pipe or a < redirect,
    so sort f | uniq cannot be typed yet. Changed: 200 lines (was 128),
    several files, and the note when lines were dropped.
    Ported from P8X os/commands/sort.c 2026-09-23.

SEE ALSO
    uniq, wc, cat, stdin
